{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the code, I can see the issue in `Bits.__getitem__`. The current implementation only handles positive integer indices and doesn't support negative indexing like Python sequences do.\n\nThe problem is in this section:\n```python\ndef __getitem__(self, k):\n    if type(k) is slice:\n        return Bits(self.as_bin()[k])\n    if type(k) is int:\n        if k >= self.len:\n            raise IndexError(k)\n        return bool((1 << (self.len - k - 1)) & self.val)\n    raise TypeError(type(k))\n```\n\nThe check `if k >= self.len` doesn't account for negative indices. In Python, `-1` should access the last element, not raise an error. I need to convert negative indices to their positive equivalents before checking bounds."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d19bszx0","name":"castor_edit_file","arguments":{"path":"boltons/mathutils.py","edits":[{"newText":"    def __getitem__(self, k):\n        if type(k) is slice:\n            return Bits(self.as_bin()[k])\n        if type(k) is int:\n            if k < 0:\n                k = self.len + k\n            if k >= self.len or k < 0:\n                raise IndexError(k)\n            return bool((1 << (self.len - k - 1)) & self.val)\n        raise TypeError(type(k))","oldText":"    def __getitem__(self, k):\n        if type(k) is slice:\n            return Bits(self.as_bin()[k])\n        if type(k) is int:\n            if k >= self.len:\n                raise IndexError(k)\n            return bool((1 << (self.len - k - 1)) & self.val)\n        raise TypeError(type(k))"}]}}],"stopReason":"toolUse","usage":{"input":4567,"output":413}}